From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: appxpackaging, wintrust, appxdeploymentclient: MSIX bundles

Most MSIX applications ship as a bundle (.msixbundle, .appxbundle): a
package per architecture, and resource packages, in one signed archive.
Nothing could read one.

- appxpackaging: IAppxBundleFactory::CreateBundleReader --
  IAppxBundleReader (footprint files, payload packages), its
  IAppxBundleManifestReader (the bundle's identity, neutral with resource
  ID "~") and IAppxBundleManifestPackageInfo for each payload package
  (type, identity, file name, offset, size). The bundle's block map is
  enforced exactly as a package's -- it must describe the archive, and every
  payload package read is checked against it -- and every package the
  manifest names must be in the bundle. The package reader's opening is
  shared (package_open). The interfaces are added to appxpackaging.idl; their
  IIDs are the published ones (Microsoft's win32metadata, as the MIT-licensed
  windows crate carries it). Resources of a payload package are not read.
- crypt32: .appxbundle and .msixbundle are AppX SIP subjects.
- wintrust: a bundle's signature names the AppX bundle SIP
  ({0f5f58b3-aade-4b9a-a434-95742d92eceb}) rather than the package one;
  its digest is the same record over the same parts. Either is accepted,
  but only for its own kind of file: a package's signature does not verify a
  bundle, nor a bundle's a package.
- appxdeploymentclient: AddPackageAsync of a bundle installs its application
  package for this machine (x64, then neutral, then x86 on x64; arm64 first
  on arm64). Resource packages are not installed yet.

Gate: make test-appx -- a bundle of an x86 and an x64 package, signed by
osslsigncode, verifies, opens, and deploys its x64 package. winget's
Microsoft-signed source still verifies (NETWORK=1).

